    blocks:[{ title:"What the rule means", html:"<p><strong>Rewriting a sinusoidal equation</strong></p><ol><li>Keep `|a|`, `k` and `c`: the height, the axis and the period don’t change.</li><li>Along one cycle the starts come in this order, a quarter period apart: sin (axis, going up) → cos (maximum) → −sin (axis, going down) → −cos (minimum) → back to sin.</li><li>A quarter period is (2π ÷ `k`) ÷ 4 = `π/(2k)`. It equals π/2 only when `k = 1`.</li><li>Adding or subtracting whole periods to `d` never changes the graph.</li></ol><p><strong>The algebra behind it:</strong> `sin θ = cos(θ - π/2)`, `cos θ = sin(θ + π/2)` and `sin θ = -sin(θ - π)`. Put `θ = k(x - d)`, then factor `k` back out: `k(x - d) - π/2 = k(x - d - π/(2k))`.</p>" }],
